Subject: [PATCH] ggml-zendnn: choose by measurement, and only where this fork wins

The adaptive fallback declines any matmul with 128 tokens or fewer. That is
right where AOCL-DLP does the work. Where BullDNN's native kernels do it the
answer is not one rule -- it depends on what else could have run the op, and
the honest version of that needed both target parts and all five
configurations to find.

Measured end to end, qwen2.5-1.5B Q4_K_M, prompt(512) / generation(128),
stock ggml against this fork:

    FX-8370E  (bdver2, no AVX2)   cpu      1.36x / 1.06x
    A10-8770E (bdver4, AVX2)      cpu      0.98x / 0.86x
    FX-8370E                      hybrid   0.27x / 1.03x
    A10-8770E                     hybrid   0.33x / 0.99x

Three rules follow, and each is there because a measurement demanded it:

1. AVX2 hosts decline everything. The CPU win is a NO-AVX2 result: without
   AVX2 ggml is confined to SSSE3 like these 128-bit kernels and they beat
   it; with AVX2 ggml is ahead and taking the op loses. Excavator has AVX2,
   Bulldozer/Piledriver/Steamroller do not.

2. With a GPU present, keep only decode. A GPU is ~4x faster at prompt sizes
   and llama.cpp sends prompt matmuls to it REGARDLESS of -ngl: at ngl=0,
   every layer CPU-resident, stock measured 257 t/s against 52 for this fork.
   So "the model does not fit in VRAM" is not a case for taking that work --
   residency is not what decides who runs it. Generation is the exception and
   is kept: it is memory-bound, the GPU has no advantage (hybrid tg measured
   1.03x and 0.99x), and the decode GEMV is ahead.

3. Two and three tokens decline. Too many rows for the one-row GEMV, and the
   GEMM pads M to its tile of four and throws half the work away (0.65x).

GGML_ZENDNN_IGNORE_GPU=1 exists because supports_op cannot tell "a GPU is in
this machine" from "a GPU is being used for this run" -- it sees registered
devices, not the scheduler's backend set, so a -dev none run on a
GPU-equipped box is indistinguishable from a hybrid one. Without the override
such a run keeps only decode and gives up the prompt win (44.62 -> 33.05 on
the FX). Set it when benchmarking or deploying CPU-only on a machine that has
a GPU.

Verified on one binary, FX-8370E:

    cpu-only, IGNORE_GPU=1    pp512  44.62   (stock 32.68)   1.37x
    hybrid,   no override     pp512 315.15   (stock 314.40)  1.00x

i.e. the win is kept where it is real and the 3.6x hybrid regression is gone.
Nothing changes on a VNNI host.

A DESTINATION-LAYOUT GUARD, added after the gate above turned a latent
backend bug into a crash. The compute path asserts the destination is
neither transposed nor permuted:

    GGML_ASSERT(nb0 == sizeof(float));
    GGML_ASSERT(nb0 <= nb1); nb1 <= nb2; nb2 <= nb3;

but supports_op only ever checked the weights and inputs for contiguity, so
an op with a permuted dst was accepted and then killed the process rather
than falling back. Opening the size gate made one reachable:
llama-perplexity aborted with SIGABRT while llama-bench, which never builds
that shape, was unaffected -- so the failure was invisible to the benchmark
that motivated the gate.

An assert in a compute path is a statement about what the caller must not
pass; the gate that admits the caller has to enforce it. With the guard,
perplexity completes and six ops are declined instead of one aborting:

    stock            PPL = 5.0436
    + this fork      PPL = 5.0888   (q4_K 3696 accepted, 6 rejected)

Q6_K IS NOW ACCEPTED, which matters more than the type count suggests. A
"Q4_K_M" model is not uniformly Q4_K -- llama.cpp puts Q6_K on the
output/lm_head and some attention tensors -- and a profile of generation on
the A10 put a THIRD of the time in ggml_vec_dot_q6_K_q8_K, work this fork
did not touch. Enabling it took the FX from 1.36x to 1.52x on prompt.

    FX-8370E, qwen2.5-1.5B Q4_K_M, CPU-only, 8 threads:
      stock              pp512 32.66   tg128 15.18   PPL 5.0436
      + fork, q4_K only        44.42         15.75       5.0888
      + fork, q4_K+q6_K        49.61         16.03       5.0537

Perplexity moves TOWARDS stock as more of the model goes through this fork,
which is the direction a correct path should move it.

One line of this took three attempts and is worth naming. The branch that
builds the params for k-quants ends in

    params.packing.ggml_type_b = std::is_same_v<TA, block_q4_K> ? 12 : 13;

so adding block_q6_K to the branch CONDITION labelled it 13 -- Q5_K -- and
the unpack then decoded Q6_K blocks as Q5_K. The result was NaN out of
llama-perplexity with everything else looking healthy: the type was
accepted, a kernel ran, matmul_direct returned success, and llama-bench
reported a plausible 1.55x because it never checks its output. Every
in-tree test passed too, because each one sets ggml_type_b = 14 by hand and
so could not exercise the branch that gets it wrong. A two-way ternary
extended by editing its condition is a trap worth remembering.


--- a/ggml/src/ggml-zendnn/ggml-zendnn.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-zendnn/ggml-zendnn.cpp
@@ -59,6 +59,8 @@
         return zendnnl::common::data_type_t::s8;
     } else if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, block_q4_K>) {
         return zendnnl::common::data_type_t::s4;
+    } else if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, block_q6_K>) {
+        return zendnnl::common::data_type_t::s8;
     } else if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, block_q5_K>) {
         return zendnnl::common::data_type_t::s8;
     } else {
@@ -85,7 +87,8 @@
